                        "entry_id": "810d7438-b14a-3965-9f44-b6cc47aa2617",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A rivet containing a hole, or a staple, on a medieval helmet, by which a camail was attached (or rarely, on another piece of armor, by which e.g. a lance rest was attached)."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "historical"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "bb7e9832-0da4-4cda-9524-d065325a925b",
                                "sense_id": "c8d31288-b1a3-34ed-8efc-6054d4787c2b",
                                "text": "... covering the neck and shoulders like a tippet; the camail was attached to the bascinet by a lace drawn through vervelles or small staples; in some examples this arrangement is distinctly exhibited, but in others the vervelles are ..."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b3e66fd0-bc57-4698-9507-e7d3442175fd",
                                "sense_id": "c8d31288-b1a3-34ed-8efc-6054d4787c2b",
                                "text": "[page 4:] And they should likewise check that they have not sharpened the vervelles of the cervellieres.\n[page 84:] The vervelles, or staples, were arranged vertically on the right-hand side of the breastplate as can clearly be seen on Real Armería reinforcing breastplate e59 and the Avant armour (figs. 26 and 17); the bracket of the lance-rest was then secured to the vervelles with a retaining pin."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a43f200d-9602-45d0-908e-4fcc02b56c13",
                                "sense_id": "c8d31288-b1a3-34ed-8efc-6054d4787c2b",
                                "text": "The bascinet is of unusually tall form, and the wearer must have had a long neck, which is an argument against the helm having been made for Joan of Arc. […] The bascinet also retains all of its original vervelles."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3546d22b-0b18-4b4c-b41e-ab82374acb80",
                                "sense_id": "c8d31288-b1a3-34ed-8efc-6054d4787c2b",
                                "text": "The Camail, or tippet of mail, which is the distinctive detail of the armour of the late fourteenth and early fifteenth century, was either hung from a flat plate of metal which was fitted over the vervelles or staples on the bascinet ..."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
